1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,795
Suppose towards a contradiction that there is a bijection

2
00:00:02,895 --> 00:00:05,031
from the naturals to the set of reals, which

3
00:00:05,131 --> 00:00:07,266
we represent as all infinite binary strings.

4
00:00:07,333 --> 00:00:09,515
Next we will focus on the diagonal, which is

5
00:00:09,615 --> 00:00:12,005
just another infinite string of 0's and 1's, and

6
00:00:12,105 --> 00:00:14,443
therefore another real number, which we call D.

7
00:00:14,533 --> 00:00:18,932
We can use this number to define a new number D star, which is just the opposite

8
00:00:19,032 --> 00:00:22,306
of D, meaning if there is a 0 we put a 1, and if there's a 1

9
00:00:22,406 --> 00:00:25,848
we put a 0. So, we obtain a new infinite string of 0's and 1's,

10
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:29,664
so by our assumption that our map was 1 to 1, this

11
00:00:29,764 --> 00:00:32,638
new real D star should exist somewhere in our list. But we

12
00:00:32,738 --> 00:00:35,664
know that it cannot be the first element in the list, since

13
00:00:35,764 --> 00:00:38,228
D^* differs from this real on teh first coordinate

14
00:00:38,266 --> 00:00:39,882
But it also cannot be the second element,

15
00:00:39,982 --> 00:00:41,640
since it differs on the second coordinate.

16
00:00:42,266 --> 00:00:46,785
It can't be the 7'th element, since it differs on the 7'th coordinate. And

17
00:00:46,885 --> 00:00:50,342
so, it cannot be equal to any of the elements in our list

18
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:52,637
But this contradicts our assumption that all real

19
00:00:52,737 --> 00:00:54,688
numbers would appear somewhere in our list.

20
00:00:54,866 --> 00:00:58,379
This argument may not convince you, in fact many mathematicians at the

21
00:00:58,479 --> 00:01:01,888
time did not accept such arguments. But note that there are multiple

22
00:01:01,988 --> 00:01:05,191
ways of proving this, and in fact this argument was not Cantor's

23
00:01:05,291 --> 00:01:09,010
original one. And in some sense, continuous sets do clearly have different

24
00:01:09,110 --> 00:01:11,746
properties from discrete ones, so it makes sense that

25
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:13,813
omega, which is the limit of some

26
00:01:13,913 --> 00:01:16,950
discrete operation, is smaller than the continuum

27
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,415
However, once we accept this fact that some infinities are larger

28
00:01:20,515 --> 00:01:23,336
then others, we enter a bit of a slippery slope, where

29
00:01:23,436 --> 00:01:26,852
we suddenly have to accept infinitely many increasing infinities.

30
00:01:26,933 --> 00:01:30,872
This follows from Cantor's theorem, which states that given any set X, the

31
00:01:30,972 --> 00:01:34,147
size of the power set is strictly larger than the size of X.

